# LeakSanitizer suppressions — THIRD-PARTY CODE ONLY.
#
# Same rule as tsan.supp: every entry is scoped to a shared object Lumen does not build.
# Patterns are substring matches against the symbolized frame, so no entry may name a
# function — only a library. A Lumen leak must never be able to match one of these.
#
# ── Mesa EGL / GL stack ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Bringing up an EGL display allocates driver-global state that Mesa never frees, and
# creating a GL context allocates gallium state with the same lifetime. Both are reachable
# for the life of the process and released only by process exit, which is exactly what LSan
# reports as a leak at exit.
#
# These resolve to a real shared object, which is why they get a proper suppression rather
# than the blunt `detect_leaks=0` the Vulkan tests need — the Vulkan ICD is dlclose'd
# before the leak check runs, so its frames degrade to "<unknown module>" and no pattern
# can match them. See TEST.md.
#
# libgallium carries its Mesa version in the filename (libgallium-26.1.6-arch1.1.so), so
# the pattern is deliberately a prefix.
leak:libEGL_mesa.so
leak:libgallium
leak:libgbm.so
leak:libglapi.so

# ── libfontconfig ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Parsing fontconfig's own system configuration (FcConfigParseAndLoadDir, run at
# FcInit from lx.text's face_cache) allocates pattern state (FcPatternObjectInsertElt,
# FcPatternAppend in fcpat.c / fcxml.c) that fontconfig keeps for the life of the
# process and frees only at exit-by-os. LSan reports it at the test's exit. Every
# frame of these leaks is fontconfig or expat parsing its XML — zero Lumen frames in
# the allocation stacks (verified across 12 runs of test_font_matches: every leak
# matched this entry, nothing remained). Reached from Lumen's faces.cppm, the same
# way the Mesa EGL entries below are reached from compositor_impl::setup_gl_present.
leak:libfontconfig.so

# ── libsystemd (sd-varlink / sd-event) ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The control server's Varlink objects retain references past full teardown: a
# standalone program with no Lumen code — sd_varlink_server + one connection +
# an sd-event loop, everything shutdown/detach/unref'd in control_server's exact
# order (/tmp/varlinkrepro) — leaks 1872 bytes in 18 allocations, the same
# all-indirect shape test_control reports (634 allocations there, every one made
# inside libsystemd code, none by Lumen). Reproduce either way:
#   LSAN_OPTIONS= ./build/asan/test_control
leak:libsystemd
